From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 14:58:24 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 70/76] fix(gastown): enumerate dotted worktrees and decide
 no-such-bead paths once (gcp-ac59)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

polecat-worktree-reap.sh had two independent blind spots, both of which let a
run report a clean pass over work it had never looked at.

DEFECT 1 — dotted sub-bead worktrees were never enumerated. The leaf whitelist
was `[a-zA-Z0-9-]`, and a split bead's id carries a dot (`feryn-derh.1`), so
every sub-bead worktree matched the reject class and hit `continue`. That drop
happens during CANDIDATE ENUMERATION — before the bulk `gc bd show`, before
`git status`, before any `record` call — so no event was emitted at all: not
pending, not skipped, not dirty. Confirmed on three rigs; winnow's patrol run
of 2026-09-02T18:28:09Z enumerated 6 of 13 per-bead worktrees and the split was
exactly on the dot. The silence is why this was P1 rather than a disk-usage
ticket: a dotted worktree holding genuinely stranded work could never surface
as `worktree_dirty_kept` either, so the blind spot hid the data-at-risk case
and not just the routine one. It also blocks gc-zxxy, whose staged rollout
reviews the logged would-reap set before flipping --no-dry-run — a set that
structurally excluded half of winnow's eligible worktrees.

The class now admits the dot but still refuses traversal: `.`, `..` and any
embedded `..` are rejected, and the leading/trailing-dot guards mirror the
existing `-*` / `*-` ones, since a bead id begins and ends with neither
separator.

DEFECT 2 — a permanently-missing bead was retried forever. Every empty-status
candidate was treated as a transient bd failure, but for a path whose basename
is not a bead id (`g7nf-base`, hand-made under `worktrees/`) the condition is
permanent, so the retry never ended and the unreapable child pinned its parent
polecat home open with it. Per the mayor's ruling the two conditions are now
separated on bd's OWN error class rather than a regex guess at id shape: bd's
stderr is captured instead of discarded, and only an id bd explicitly reported
as `no issue found matching "<id>"`, from a read that ran to completion, is
treated as permanent. Anything else stays transient, which is the conservative
direction — the cost of being wrong is one more retry, never a removal.

A permanent verdict is logged as `worktree_no_such_bead` and then decided on
the worktree's own evidence, since no bead closure will ever authorise it.
Gates 2 and 4 need a bead and cannot bind, so the path must clear gate 3 (clean
tree) plus a new gate 5: HEAD reachable from a remote-tracking ref, i.e. the
content exists somewhere other than this directory. That is what made
g7nf-base a leak rather than lost work. Failing gate 5 is surfaced as
`worktree_unpublished_kept` — a finding, not a silent retry. Gate 5 is
deliberately scoped to this path only; the closed-bead path stays ungated on
publication for the reason the header already gives (a rebase-merging refinery
rewrites hashes), and bead closure remains the better proof there.

Also fixed a consequence of the same defect: bd exits 1 and prints an error
OBJECT rather than an array when NO id in a batch resolves, which the array
check read as a dead store. A rig whose only candidate was a no-such-bead path
aborted the entire cycle as `worktree_bead_query_failed`, pointing the reader
at a Dolt server that was answering perfectly. That batch is now normalised to
an empty array and the per-candidate gates decide, exactly as in a mixed batch.

Validation: three new tests, each verified RED against the pre-fix script on
its own defect and green after — dotted enumeration (including that traversal
leaves are still refused AND produce no log line), the permanent-vs-transient
split across published/unpublished/dirty/store-error, and the all-missing
batch. The `gc bd` stub was made faithful to real bd's three observable
behaviours for a missing id, since a stub that silently omits an id tests a bd
that does not exist; that required updating the existing unreadable-bead case
to use a fuzzy hit, which is the real shape of a per-id transient failure
inside a successful batch. Full suite green, shellcheck clean.

Does not address gcp-actg (polecat HOME worktrees, excluded by the
`*/polecats/*/worktrees/*` path gate) — different mechanism, separate bead.
